Here's the corrected code and the solution to your problem:

**Problem Statement:**

Write a Java program that takes an HTML string as input, parses it using Jsoup, and extracts the text content.

**Solution:**

```java
import org.jsoup.Jsoup;
import org.jsoup.nodes.Document;

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String html = "<html><head></head><body>Hello</body></html>";
        Document doc = Jsoup.parse(html);
        System.out.println(doc.body().text());
    }
}
```

**Explanation:**

*   We import the required classes from Jsoup library.
*   In the `main` method, we define an HTML string and parse it using `Jsoup.parse`.
*   The `doc` object now contains the parsed HTML document.
*   Finally, we print the text content of the parsed HTML document.

**Example Output:**

When you run this program with the provided HTML string, it will output:

```
Hello
```

This is because the `body()` method of the `Document` object returns the text content of the body element in the HTML document.

**Tips and Variations:**

*   To extract specific elements from the parsed HTML using their tag names or IDs:
    ```java
Document doc = Jsoup.parse(html).select("h1").text();
```
    This code will extract all `h1` elements from the parsed HTML and print their text content.
    ```java
Document doc = Jsoup.parse(html);
doc.select("b").first().text();  // prints the first bold element's text content
```
